    "difficultyReason": "The stimulus blends a general medication trend with a specific subgroup exception. The correct answer is a hedged comparative inference. Trap answers either drop the subgroup qualifier (scope creep), add an absolute claim not warranted by the data, or invert the direction of support for the exception group.",
    "stimulus": "A large regional health database tracking outcomes for patients with chronic lower-back pain found that patients who underwent surgery were, on average, able to return to work approximately three weeks sooner than patients who received physical therapy alone. However, among patients over 65, those who received physical therapy returned to work at roughly the same rate as younger surgery patients, while surgery patients over 65 took, on average, nearly twice as long to return to work as their younger counterparts. The database also noted that physical therapy was the sole treatment for roughly 40 percent of all chronic lower-back pain patients in the study.",
    "questionStem": "The information above most strongly supports which one of the following claims?",
    "choices": [
      {
        "label": "A",
        "text": "Surgery is never an appropriate treatment for chronic lower-back pain in patients over 65."
      },
      {
        "label": "B",
        "text": "For patients over 65 with chronic lower-back pain, physical therapy is likely to produce a faster return to work than surgery."
      },
      {
        "label": "C",
        "text": "Chronic lower-back pain surgery produces worse outcomes than physical therapy for all age groups."
      },
      {
        "label": "D",
        "text": "Physical therapy is a more cost-effective treatment for chronic lower-back pain than surgery."
      },
      {
        "label": "E",
        "text": "Younger patients who undergo surgery for chronic lower-back pain have longer recovery times than any other patient group studied."
      }
    ],
    "correctAnswer": "B",
    "explanations": {
      "correct": "The stimulus states that among patients over 65, physical therapy patients returned to work at roughly the same rate as younger surgery patients, while surgery patients over 65 took nearly twice as long as their younger counterparts. This directly supports the hedged claim that, for the over-65 group, physical therapy is likely to produce a faster return to work than surgery.",
      "A": "Too strong — the stimulus describes return-to-work timelines but does not say surgery is 'never appropriate' for patients over 65. This is the too-strong trap.",
      "B": "Correct. The data specifically show that surgery patients over 65 took nearly twice as long to return to work as younger surgery patients, while physical therapy patients over 65 fared comparably to younger surgery patients — supporting a hedged conclusion favoring physical therapy for that group.",
      "C": "Scope creep — the surgical advantage for the general population is stated explicitly; the passage only shows a reversal for the over-65 subgroup, not 'all age groups.'",
      "D": "Plausible but unsupported — cost-effectiveness is never mentioned in the stimulus. This is the plausible-but-unsupported trap.",
      "E": "Opposite direction — the stimulus says surgery patients over 65 took nearly twice as long as younger surgery patients, meaning younger surgery patients had shorter, not longer, recovery times relative to older surgery patients."
    },
    "tags": ["most_strongly_supported", "medicine", "subgroup_exception", "hedged_inference", "scope_creep_trap"]
  },
